{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"8712e5cb-5c17-4f78-8159-fe8e322bd52d","hash":"efcdc3d5ec21d44bf09ce1b14dba7930abee8d78eb45e8fead0700c9e9e9a02a","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The current faces look mannequin-like — glossy flat skin, glassy eyes, painted brows, and a floating chin strap — with tubular bodies and pipe-like limbs. I'll start a background Blender pass to fix the faces and bodies while keeping rig, animations, and reload timing untouched, and continue the ragdoll play-test in the meantime.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CNmVecefxryJjkvJhEVJzM","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Soldier face and body realism","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":true,"prompt":"You are doing the v4 realism pass on the WW2 soldier models of a browser game (three.js r185). The user asked: \"enhance soldiers - make their face more realistic and their body too\".\n\n## Where things are\nWorktree root: /Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/.claude/worktrees/hedgerow — the game is in fun/hedgerow/. Work only inside fun/hedgerow/.\n- Builder: fun/hedgerow/tools/blender/build_soldier.py (865 lines) and fun/hedgerow/tools/blender/soldier_parts.py (~5000 lines). Shared helpers you may import but must NOT edit: tools/blender/kit.py, bake.py (bake_model with a `scale_islands` hook you already use via soldier_parts.island_scaler), surfaces.py. Register any new surface recipes at run time from soldier_parts (as register_surfaces() already does).\n- Notes: fun/hedgerow/tools/blender/notes/soldier.md — read it first; it documents the rig, bones, axes, clips, reload event frames, sockets, budgets and the bake. Contract docs: fun/hedgerow/docs/models-brief.md and models-brief-v2.md.\n- Output: fun/hedgerow/assets/models/soldier.glb (now 4.8 MB, outfits `allied` 21.7k tris and `axis` 22.4k tris, each ONE skinned mesh with ONE baked material, base 2048², mr 2048², normal 1024²). weapons.glb is built by the same script; leave the weapons unchanged unless needed (if you rebuild it, it must stay identical in names, origins and sockets).\n- Previews: fun/hedgerow/tools/blender/previews/soldier/ (gitignored, never commit). The current face close-ups `allied_rest_face.png` / `axis_rest_face.png` and `*_rest_front.png` show the problem.\n- Run Blender headless only: `/Applications/Blender.app/Contents/MacOS/Blender --background --factory-startup --python fun/hedgerow/tools/blender/build_soldier.py [-- soldier|weapons] [nopreview]` from the worktree root. Long builds: run them in the background and keep working.\n\n## What is wrong now (from the previews)\nFace: reads as a shop mannequin. Glossy plastic skin of one flat orange-pink tone; grey glassy eyes with no visible sclera/iris structure; thin painted eyebrows; soft, featureless cheeks and jaw; shiny lips; long thin tubular neck; the US helmet chin strap hangs in the air well away from the cheek. Body: tubular limbs, narrow shoulders, thin arms and legs like pipes, boxy torso; the uniform reads as a smooth shell over a mannequin.\n\n## Goals\nFace (both outfits; the head is shared):\n- Anatomy: a believable adult male head — cheekbones, jaw angle and chin, temples, brow ridge with eye sockets that shadow the eyes, nasolabial folds, philtrum, defined lips with a vermilion border, upper and lower eyelids with thickness and a lid crease, tear duct, proper ears, Adam's apple, neck muscles; neck thickness and length of a fit 20–25 year old (~37–40 cm collar). Keep stature ~1.745 m at the skull and the eye height close to now (the rifle sight line in notes must stay within ~1.5 cm of the aiming eye — check and report).\n- Eyes: off-white sclera with faint veins and pink corners, coloured iris with a darker limbal ring, black pupil, a glossy cornea (low roughness only on the eye), set back under the lids.\n- Skin: matte-to-satin (roughness ~0.45–0.65, a little lower on nose/forehead), warm variation (red cheeks, nose, ears; yellower forehead; blue-grey stubble on jaw and upper lip), pores and fine wrinkles (crow's feet, forehead lines) in the normal map, grime/dirt and sweat for a soldier in the field. Eyebrows with hair-like density, not lines.\n- Variety: add 3 shape keys on the head that export as glTF morph targets on each outfit mesh, named exactly `face_broad`, `face_gaunt`, `face_older` (a heavier wider face; a lean hollow-cheeked one; an older one with heavier lids, deeper folds, sagging jaw). Each must look right at weight 1 and blend sanely with the others. They must not move the eyes off the sight line by more than ~1 cm. Report the morph target index order. (I will wire random weights per soldier in the engine.)\n- Chin straps: US M1 helmet strap realistically either fastened under the chin or hooked up over the rim at the back; German strap under the chin or tucked. No straps floating in air; they must not cut through the face in any clip (check the animation previews).\n\nBody (both outfits):\n- Anatomically plausible proportions under the cloth: broader shoulders (deltoids), chest depth, upper-arm/forearm taper to the wrist, hands with knuckles, fingernails and tendons, thighs tapering to the knee, calves. No pipe limbs.\n- Cloth: wool/cotton drape with gravity folds, fabric thickness at hems, cuffs and pocket edges, bunching at elbows/knees/waist (keep what exists, improve), weave in the normal map, wear, sweat and mud on the lower legs, darker damp areas.\n- Skinning: deformation must hold up in every clip — shoulders when aiming and throwing, elbows, hips when crouching, knees. Add edge loops at joints and check volume (no candy-wrapper elbows, no collapsing shoulders). Render the existing per-clip previews and look at them.\n\n## Hard constraints (the game depends on these — do not change them)\n- Node names and hierarchy: `rig` (armature root), `allied` and `axis` skinned meshes as children of `rig`, `socket_weapon` (child of joint `hand_r`) and `socket_weapon_l` (child of `hand_l`) with their current transforms. Each outfit stays ONE skinned mesh with ONE baked material named after it (`allied`, `axis`), WebP textures, COLOR_0 kept (flat white is fine), ≤4 weights per vertex, normalised.\n- Bones: same 20 bone names, hierarchy, rest pose, bone axes and lengths (ragdoll.js and character.js read them; see notes). Animation clips: same names, frame counts, fps and motion; the rig's extras `reload_events` unchanged. You may re-weight but not re-animate.\n- Front = +Z three.js (−Y Blender), origin at the feet.\n- Budgets: soldier.glb ≤ 7 MB; each outfit ≤ 32k tris. The base map may go to 4096² only if the face and hands really need it and the file still fits; otherwise keep 2048² and give the face/hands more texels with island scaling.\n- Deterministic builds (fixed seeds).\n- Do not commit anything, do not open a browser or start any server. Do not edit kit.py, bake.py, surfaces.py, any JS, or other sets' builders.\n\n## When done\n1. Verify the GLB with a script: nodes, bone names/order, clip names and durations, morph target names on both meshes, tris, texture sizes, file size.\n2. Run the game's node checks from the worktree root and report their output: `node fun/hedgerow/tests/character-check.mjs`, `node fun/hedgerow/tests/ragdoll-check.mjs`, `node fun/hedgerow/tests/world-check.mjs`, `node fun/hedgerow/tests/route-check.mjs`, `node fun/hedgerow/tests/distance-check.mjs`.\n3. Render new previews: face close-ups (front, 3/4, profile) for both outfits at rest and with each shape key, plus the existing clip previews; look at them yourself and fix what reads wrong before reporting.\n4. Update notes/soldier.md (a \"v4\" section: what changed, tris, textures, morph targets and their index order, the sight-line check).\n5. Report back: file size, tris per outfit, what changed for face and body, the morph target names and order, anything you could not do, and the preview file names worth looking at."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
