{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"8712e5cb-5c17-4f78-8159-fe8e322bd52d","hash":"a3246d175a6123dac925313cf70904dcc4ddae08831a1d54ea30f12c08a89640","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AJFChRZ3vVHiGJhGsxm8aK","type":"tool_result","content":"208\t* **New dressing keys:**\n209\t  * `ashlar`: Caen-stone quoins, jambs, lintels, sills, string courses,\n210\t    voussoirs and coping, with tooling, pits and chipped arrises.\n211\t  * `ashlar_step`: worn treads, no moss.\n212\t  * `fieldstone`: single proud or loose stones.\n213\t  * `stone_rubble`, `fieldstone_fresh`: rubble heaps that stay moss-free.\n214\t* **Render.** `plaster` and `plaster_dirty` now bake as lime render:\n215\t  * blotches and trowel texture;\n216\t  * crazing and long cracks;\n217\t  * blown patches with a broken rim showing the rubble;\n218\t  * vertical run-off stains;\n219\t  * algae and splash at the foot.\n220\t* **Glass.** `glass_dirty` is the default for every window: dust film, rain\n221\t  streaks and grime caked in the corners.\n222\t* **Paint.** Shutters (`shutter_*`) and doors (`door_*`) flake at the edges\n223\t  down to grey wood. Doors are also worn at handle height and rotting at\n224\t  the bottom.\n225\t* **North slopes.** Gable roofs give their back slope (+Y) the mossy\n226\t  variant: `roof_tile_n`, `roof_slate_n` or `thatch_n`, with heavy moss and\n227\t  lichen."}]}}
