{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"8712e5cb-5c17-4f78-8159-fe8e322bd52d","hash":"800d867d5d532b736d3d61e14dc1f28a8dbaf8f40dfd5e5fa1557aef1fd1f547","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Halving resolution only saves 0–4 ms, confirming the bottleneck is geometry rather than pixels—pointing to enemies rendered regardless of visibility and near trees as the likely culprits. I'll measure each of these per view next.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AiSh9quakM46TfgTCoAuU2","name":"mcp__Claude_Browser__javascript_tool","input":{"action":"javascript_exec","text":"const G = window.hedgerow.G;\nconst cfg = await import('/fun/hedgerow/js/config.js');\nconst res = [];\ntoggleEnemies(false); res.push(await viewGpu('no enemies')); toggleEnemies(true);\nconst saved = Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(cfg.QUALITY).map(([k, q]) => [k, q.treeNear]));\nfor (const q of Object.values(cfg.QUALITY)) q.treeNear = 36;\nawait new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 300));\nres.push(await viewGpu('trees near 36 m'));\nfor (const [k, v] of Object.entries(saved)) cfg.QUALITY[k].treeNear = v;\n// enemies in front of the camera only (what frustum culling would draw)\nconst THREE = await import('three');\nconst fr = new THREE.Frustum(), pv = new THREE.Matrix4(), sp = new THREE.Sphere();\nwindow.cullEnemiesNow = () => {\n  G.camera.updateMatrixWorld(); pv.multiplyMatrices(G.camera.projectionMatrix, G.camera.matrixWorldInverse); fr.setFromProjectionMatrix(pv);\n  let n = 0;\n  for (const e of G.enemies) { sp.center.set(e.pos.x, e.pos.y + 1, e.pos.z); sp.radius = 1.6; const v = fr.intersectsSphere(sp); for (const m of e.char.meshes) m.visible = v; n += v; }\n  return n;\n};\nconst out = [];\nfor (const [k, [x, z, yaw, pitch]] of Object.entries(VIEWS)) {\n  window.hedgerow.flyTo(x, G.terrain.height(x, z) + 1.7, z, yaw, pitch);\n  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 700));\n  const total = G.enemies.filter((e) => e.char.root.visible).length;\n  const inView = cullEnemiesNow();\n  out.push(`${k} ${(await gpuTime(15)).toFixed(2)} (${inView}/${total} drawn)`);\n  toggleEnemies(true);\n}\nres.push('enemies culled to view: ' + out.join(' | '));\nres.join('\\n')","tabId":"seed"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"8712e5cb-5c17-4f78-8159-fe8e322bd52d","hash":"7900f89d3cc73ed2c7c27b80077794a47955f7c80c38c601ce3a467ff4bf39d5","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AiSh9quakM46TfgTCoAuU2","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"no enemies: start 6.16 | street 6.08 | square 5.67 | forest 8.39 | trench 7.61 ms (pr 1)\\ntrees near 36 m: start 8.69 | street 9.01 | square 7.37 | forest 8.33 | trench 7.59 ms (pr 1)\\nenemies culled to view: start 7.97 (46/22 drawn) | street 6.74 (38/22 drawn) | square 7.00 (35/22 drawn) | forest 8.13 (20/22 drawn) | trench 7.50 (13/22 drawn)\""},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nTab Context:\n- Executed on tabId: seed\n- Available tabs:\n  • tabId seed: \"Hedgerow — Saint-Clair at dusk\" (\"http://localhost:8765/fun/hedgerow/?debug&god&at=0,72&go\")"}]}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"8712e5cb-5c17-4f78-8159-fe8e322bd52d","hash":"411f4eb69b98ed541961543b537fe0235ae76b186aa9d855ab7d6b14aca91201","message":{"role":"system","content":"<total_tokens>14946135 tokens left</total_tokens>"}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"8712e5cb-5c17-4f78-8159-fe8e322bd52d","hash":"fb565dc9601ee7cf0839a952396dcb80da3e7088be81eb8f6a68b7a24e9221a2","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The measurements reveal three wins: culling off-screen enemies, a low-detail soldier mesh for distant units, and a thinned mid-distance tree model — plus I'll add automatic resolution scaling for weaker GPUs/high-DPI screens. Starting the two Blender asset jobs in the background now, then moving to the engine-side culling work.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ByWBUZzcMsG5F8hDqBV8Ep","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"ae51200f68617c8f0","summary":"Add distant LOD meshes to the soldiers","message":"New soldier task (performance): add a low-detail LOD mesh per outfit to soldier.glb. Profiling shows each in-view enemy costs a 29k-tri skinned + 3-morph mesh even at 100 m; ~20 are in view at once in the village.\n\nAdd `allied_lod` and `axis_lod`: skinned mesh nodes, children of `rig`, bound to the SAME skin (same 20 joints and inverse bind matrices as the full meshes), using the SAME glTF material as the full mesh of that outfit (`allied` / `axis` — share the material and its images, do not duplicate textures). Target ~5–6k tris each, made from the final baked/UV'd mesh by decimation that preserves UV seams and boundaries (so the baked texture maps exactly), keeps the silhouette (helmet, shoulders, webbing, pack, boots; arms and hands still readable for aiming poses), no interior/hidden parts. Skin weights ≤4 per vertex, normalised (transfer from the full skinned mesh, interpolated), COLOR_0 kept (flat white is fine), NO morph targets. Everything else stays exactly as now: full meshes, morph targets and their order, rig, bones, clips, reload_events, sockets, node names; weapons.glb byte-identical (restore it if the build re-exports). soldier.glb ≤ 7 MB (it is 6.30 MB now). Deterministic.\n\nNote: I am about to run a nature build in parallel (mid-distance tree LODs), so your bake will share the CPU; don't start a second soldier build while one runs. No commits, no browser, no server; don't edit kit.py/bake.py/surfaces.py/JS.\n\nVerify by script (both LOD nodes: parent `rig`, skin index same as the full mesh's, material index same as the full mesh's, tris, weights, no targets; everything else unchanged vs the current GLB — keep a copy of the current soldier.glb to compare). Render previews: full vs LOD side by side for both outfits at 10 m and 30 m in rest, aim, run and death_fwd; look at them. Run the five node checks. Update notes/soldier.md (v4.2) and the MODELS.md soldier row. Report tris per LOD, file size, and anything that looks wrong."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Knncw1T28zKQWc58yrBc3t","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"a7113d61792f1e069","summary":"Add mid-distance tree LODs to nature","message":"New nature task (performance), from the user's \"improve performance\" request. In the browser the forest view spends ~2 ms of GPU per frame on the full trees inside the 52 m near range (pine_big/fir_big ~7.8k tris, others 3.7–5.2k, a few hundred in range), and the near→impostor swap can't move closer without visible popping. So the engine will get a third tier: full model up to ~22–28 m, a new MID model from there to the near limit (~52 m), the existing `_lod` impostor beyond.\n\nAdd a `<name>_mid` node for every tree that has a `_lod`: pine_a, pine_b, pine_c, oak_a, oak_b, birch_a, tree_dead, pine_big, fir_big, oak_big, beech_big.\n- Same origin, orientation and scale as the full node; visual only (no colliders/anchors needed).\n- About 25–30 % of the full tris: trunk and main limbs decimated keeping the silhouette, fine twigs dropped; leaf/needle cards thinned to ~35–45 % of the count but enlarged (~1.3–1.5×, area-preserving) so crown coverage, density, colour and silhouette match the full tree from 25–55 m. Cards keep their crown-out normals and stay alphaMode MASK.\n- Reuse the existing materials and atlases (the full tree's bark material, the shared card atlases) — do not duplicate images. Every primitive keeps POSITION/NORMAL/TEXCOORD_0/COLOR_0 (card occlusion in COLOR_0 as now).\n- nature.glb ≤ 16 MB (13.4 MB now). Deterministic. Everything else unchanged (all existing nodes byte-for-byte equivalent in geometry/materials/extras).\n\nNote: a soldier build is running in parallel, so bakes share the CPU. No commits, no browser, no server; don't edit kit.py/bake.py/surfaces.py/JS.\n\nVerify by script (every `_mid` node present, tris vs full, materials shared, attributes, alpha modes; existing nodes unchanged — keep a copy of the current nature.glb to compare). Previews: full vs mid side by side at 30 m and 45 m for each tree, in dim dusk-like light, plus a forest scene; look at them and iterate so the swap at ~25 m would be hard to notice. fix_card_alpha runs in the build. Run the five node checks. Update notes/nature.md and the MODELS.md nature row. Report the tris per mid node, file size, and anything that doesn't land."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
